{0 Forms and file uploads}

{!Workero.Request.form_data} reads a [multipart/form-data] or
[application/x-www-form-urlencoded] body. It gives a {!Workero.Form_data.t}.

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match Request.form_data req with
  | form -> (
      match Form_data.get form "avatar" with
      | Some (File file) ->
          Response.text
            (Printf.sprintf "%s: %d bytes\n" (Form_data.file_name file)
               (Form_data.file_size file))
      | Some (Text _) -> Response.text ~status:400 "avatar must be a file\n"
      | None -> Response.text ~status:400 "no avatar\n")
  | exception Js_error _ -> Response.text ~status:400 "not a form\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.Request.form_data} raises {!Workero.Js_error} when the content type is
neither of the two, or when the body is malformed.

{1 Values}

A field is a {!Workero.Form_data.value}: [Text of string] or [File of file].

- {!Workero.Form_data.get} gives the first entry with a name.
- {!Workero.Form_data.get_all} gives all entries with a name.
- {!Workero.Form_data.to_list} gives all entries in the order sent. Duplicate
  names stay.

{1 Files}

- {!Workero.Form_data.file_name} is the file name that the client sent.
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_type} is the [content-type] of the part, or [""].
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_size} is the size in bytes.
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_bytes} reads the whole file into a [string].
- {!Workero.Form_data.file_stream} gives the file as a {!Workero.Stream.t}.
  Use it to write a large file to {{!page-r2}R2} without loading it.

{1 Limit}

The runtime parses the whole body before it gives you the form, so an upload is
in memory. {!Workero.Form_data.file_stream} only reads from that copy. To handle
an upload that does not fit in memory, read {!Workero.Request.body} in chunks.
See {{!page-streams}Streams}.
